Let me understand the bug more deeply. In the original (correct) code:

```js
if (pos.xOffset || pos.yOffset) {
  // Flush the current buffer
  flush(i);
  // Move the text position and flush just the current character
  this.addContent(`1 0 0 1 ${number(x + pos.xOffset * scale)} ${number(y + pos.yOffset * scale)} Tm`);
  flush(i + 1);
  hadOffset = true;
} else {
  // If the last character had an offset, reset the text position
  if (hadOffset) {
    this.addContent(`1 0 0 1 ${number(x)} ${number(y)} Tm`);
    hadOffset = false;
  }
  // Group segments that don't have any advance adjustments
  if (pos.xAdvance - pos.advanceWidth !== 0) {
    addSegment(i + 1);
  }
}
x += pos.xAdvance * scale;
```

So when a glyph HAS an offset (e.g., a combining mark), we:
1. Flush the current buffer (emit accumulated text up to i)
2. Move text position to x+xOffset, y+yOffset and emit just this glyph
3. Set hadOffset = true

When a glyph does NOT have an offset:
1. If previous had offset, reset text matrix to (x, y) — the current pen position — and clear hadOffset
2. If the advance differs from advanceWidth (kerning), break the segment at i+1

The key insight: after an offset glyph is emitted at a special position, the next normal glyph needs the text matrix reset to the proper pen position (x, y) because x has been advanced by xAdvance but the Tm was set to the offset position. The reset uses the current `x` which already includes the advance from the offset glyph.

In the buggy version, the branches were swapped, so:
- Normal glyphs (no offset) were treated as offset glyphs: flush, move to x+xOffset (which is x+0 = x, but still emits individually), set hadOffset=true. This causes EVERY normal glyph to be emitted individually with a Tm reset! That's why "New york" became single characters.
- Offset glyphs were treated as normal: just reset if hadOffset and group segments. This means combining marks wouldn't be positioned correctly.

This explains all the symptoms. My fix restores the correct logic.

Now let me think about the oblique fix. The original:
```js
if (typeof options.oblique === 'number') {
  skew = -Math.tan((options.oblique * Math.PI) / 180);
} else {
  skew = -0.25;
}
```

When oblique is a number (angle in degrees), use tan. When oblique is boolean true, use default -0.25. The bug swapped these, so a number would give -0.25 and a boolean would try `options.oblique * Math.PI` = true * Math.PI = Math.PI, giving tan(PI) = 0, skew = 0 (no skew). That's also a regression.

